#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "202b0e22-7529-5696-9e15-18ed74e11d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-8882" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\:  5\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: 4\\cos\\phi\\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{36}+\\dfrac{y^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 6X, \\qquad\ny =5 Y, \\qquad\nz = 4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\nY = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nZ = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6X,5Y,4Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}6\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e45977-275f-51ef-b8b5-f7a47c770ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Sphere Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "cd3d5e9a-ef62-5239-af97-1c6353f75f4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8883" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 5 \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\text{_____}, \\: 5 \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2+y^2+z^2=25.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5 \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171798 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "150bfb70-706c-53d0-8a5c-1da52f27ad3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8884" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 6r\\sin\\theta, \\: r \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz^2=\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{y^2}{36}, \\qquad z\\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 5X, \\qquad\ny = 6Y, \\qquad\nz = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\nZ^2=X^2+Y^2,\\qquad Z\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z = r.$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,6Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5r\\cos\\theta}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a77a817c-5081-574d-bd79-f85393947868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372772} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d3b715-4c3a-5bc3-9bbf-ef03fae08ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372773} {:answer/id #uuid "720c8284-2721-5421-9af7-b90ec2c69fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372774} {:answer/id #uuid "4aff7442-93fb-56f7-bff0-a8720b1a8ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372775} {:answer/id #uuid "7028f727-56af-5ae1-89ce-61d4294db35e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d3b715-4c3a-5bc3-9bbf-ef03fae08ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372773} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefd1bd8-b58d-5072-9751-9b723f63c8d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e40041d8-fcdc-5773-bf4e-3e3bd1e930ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-103978" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "In cylindrical polar coordinates, the given half-cone has the equation\n\n$$\nz=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(x,y,z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e83c31e-61ba-532a-ac3a-1497b32c7003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168122} {:answer/id #uuid "df9f97a6-97f5-57f5-87ec-6a0badff5d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168123} {:answer/id #uuid "e79681a0-7526-5c40-b33d-8b7ac8cc8344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168124} {:answer/id #uuid "4a810fe9-06f1-5fb5-908d-19f89a367ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168125} {:answer/id #uuid "5e04b0d0-6f61-5468-91b6-4a2a7f10dd7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e04b0d0-6f61-5468-91b6-4a2a7f10dd7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168126} :answer-field/id #uuid "10892380-0261-5e00-95fb-9c294ae3cb70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7902ab3b-1269-59a0-b777-47bd4ce0abe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103979" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2},\\,Z\\ge 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z=r.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e45977-275f-51ef-b8b5-f7a47c770ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Sphere Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed031848-9011-5f6f-b9f4-9c1c4a725336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372779} {:answer/id #uuid "8749cbc7-8c9d-5205-9b5a-150ad2307b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372780} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a0c8d3-4264-5842-b8cd-30441d13205d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372781} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb6076-5cdd-5195-aa0e-abda749a07d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372782} {:answer/id #uuid "2c88d89b-7ab8-5330-b770-0b23c54ba357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6a0c8d3-4264-5842-b8cd-30441d13205d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372781} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d94636c-625f-5994-b3c1-f8818ab6f729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c45a05a-f78d-57b9-b6e6-41b9f8f5a198" :question/math-academy-id "q-105617" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e45977-275f-51ef-b8b5-f7a47c770ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Sphere Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce76758c-f0f1-5482-8c5f-6828ac483688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372786} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7795a3-09e7-501a-b723-17992f53c5d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372787} {:answer/id #uuid "123eda46-cbc4-574a-a806-3511b0375d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372788} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3526ba-90ea-5890-a502-677ccbc9aad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372789} {:answer/id #uuid "59734bbe-ed68-58c8-ae5e-2cea6170bac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f3526ba-90ea-5890-a502-677ccbc9aad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372789} :answer-field/id #uuid "a35569a9-83bd-5c0b-b575-09c8d5aec211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3af26f3-6e90-5e80-9417-4c743f059326" :question/math-academy-id "q-105629" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e45977-275f-51ef-b8b5-f7a47c770ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Sphere Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1297dea6-c0dd-55dc-b64a-b2e449483ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372793} {:answer/id #uuid "a702befe-6a53-5908-97ea-b527847cf98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372794} {:answer/id #uuid "1c309398-04de-5ad1-886e-6a8d1988232d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372795} {:answer/id #uuid "3365f326-f5c2-5dde-ab70-4e363ca8ca61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372796} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d65fea-f171-5213-af43-3edc50c9a84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1297dea6-c0dd-55dc-b64a-b2e449483ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372793} :answer-field/id #uuid "c57a638b-5c42-5bf5-b5f9-174bfb424f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd8b2461-8779-5c0a-ba68-390b32daf6de" :question/math-academy-id "q-105631" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=49.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $7$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =7.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372798 :knowledge-poi