nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e45977-275f-51ef-b8b5-f7a47c770ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Sphere Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c1373e1-1ad8-5fee-8534-82de5ad69c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372807} {:answer/id #