ian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc8e2196-cd2d-5356-bf77-09c2b8be9228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372814} {:answer/id #uuid "a56189f9-f1a3-5391-bd91-16e1e6f643ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372815} {:answer/id #uuid "82f3a81b-4486-5a7e-81a2-81f336324494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372816} {:answer/id #uuid "7325b847-a70b-5af1-a0f5-a981b3f7f805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372817} {:answer/id #uuid "268b91a5-ed38-54af-a3c2-39060e86eba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8e2196-cd2d-5356-bf77-09c2b8be9228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372814} :answer-field/id #uuid "d195ddf4-74ba-5cc6-ac50-e5851f570c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1923526-f8b0-5c49-bd76-936fcf5d6c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-105660" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da028a19-6f55-58e2-a486-9db867350cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372821} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2a36fb-1b99-57fe-8897-e95cdbc7e20e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372822} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8b3232-5062-5fe7-ac05-94d9cf943e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372823} {:answer/id #uuid "859ef6c1-acc8-5d86-b1b2-f307d1dec1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372824} {:answer/id #uuid "e29d2837-cefd-5e29-9a5f-655df7aa26c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e29d2837-cefd-5e29-9a5f-655df7aa26c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372825} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f5bd1b-aa21-5600-b54f-5e5e21e74fe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd39f0e9-192d-5f90-97dc-80c9c45019ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-105662" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+4{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}Y,\\,z=5Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,\\frac{1}{2}Y,5Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cbbe258-c635-5700-8203-0a49951ddec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372828} {:answer/id #uuid "8811ee64-af87-51e9-8e4e-c849160f01a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372829} {:answer/id #uuid "edc61c5a-283e-5131-bb9e-a0c2ba573d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372830} {:answer/id #uuid "22c31fe8-b54c-57bc-9bae-94d9d0652902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372831} {:answer/id #uuid "c688b832-5aec-5f19-9ae1-e8a76d5d33b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c688b832-5aec-5f19-9ae1-e8a76d5d33b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372832} :answer-field/id #uuid "74d01d4b-6a8f-5295-8018-3a01f09bae90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de032730-b4c9-509d-a255-b54cc05b2476" :question/math-academy-id "q-105686" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=6X,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6X,3Y,4Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa39841c-2dbb-5881-bbd1-0510f3d8d399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372835} {:answer/id #uuid "adf6c505-24d5-5bb2-bbda-63753da1523f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372836} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2307d1-369a-5c08-82ed-2b05a829d9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372837} {:answer/id #uuid "303286a2-8e8c-575b-bfc8-44fea5756b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372838} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7ff029-8f5b-522d-aab0-e844bc2e0803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb2307d1-369a-5c08-82ed-2b05a829d9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372837} :answer-field/id #uuid "308d7fef-c61c-5727-a963-73e5edbf67e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5674745-e1c6-5f88-ac0e-f59c0cf03027" :question/math-academy-id "q-105689" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+25{z}^{2}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=\\frac{1}{5}Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,2Y,\\frac{1}{5}Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of an Ellipsoid Using Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf901aa-e246-5be9-9c88-6a526f217fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372842} {:answer/id #uuid "ee843141-7924-59b5-82d9-89b79f320015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372843} {:answer/id #uuid "1d780782-c04e-5d59-a9dd-22cd1f03ea4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372844} {:answer/id #uuid "e754928a-5bd7-5a6c-af64-9940fe0d1ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372845} {:answer/id #uuid "bf17a6f7-aea2-511c-b189-df7e216609ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d780782-c04e-5d59-a9dd-22cd1f03ea4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372844} :answer-field/id #uuid "9553b8a3-8d62-5e13-b72c-acc330ae1364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95cac1d1-11d7-5a1b-b4e7-d28d5113fbb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105691" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=3Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,2Y,3Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5dab4f3-5b4e-5d5d-bf3a-c4d197526781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{4}\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168164} {:answer/id #uuid "e18ff035-aebf-577f-b395-dc2ef86780d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168165} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a12c48-5a62-5928-978e-42baf881dcc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168166} {:answer/id #uuid "0422b4d5-cced-5f80-ae5b-5ba6ecb6bb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cos 4θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168167} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4a879e-df26-5131-8503-3f08480febf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{4}\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6a12c48-5a62-5928-978e-42baf881dcc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168166} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f40d00c-77a4-51ec-97b9-d41c575750d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5888ac9-e652-5536-bba8-b7c51e45c946" :question/math-academy-id "q-105722" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2},\\,Z\\ge 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z=r.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\,4Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c191af6c-8ef1-5003-9cb8-aa96913d3ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372849} {:answer/id #uuid "069ad6d6-ea15-5d4e-b129-a9de8da70b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372850} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7a243b-d0fc-5328-9537-30a1c5a1103c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372851} {:answer/id #uuid "8250f191-2d28-557f-a9c6-e7ac9eb5f40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372852} {:answer/id #uuid "f286c076-05a7-5735-98f6-f8ba54bbee4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d7a243b-d0fc-5328-9537-30a1c5a1103c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372851} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9e3cde-3066-5e27-9fe0-8a02efedd981" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a0d0d94-b63d-5b48-bbbe-77b678514a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105725" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=4Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3X,\\,4Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Cone Using Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f550f9b-dfdb-521b-b9b8-16062d6f405a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372856} {:answer/id #uuid "33ae0254-d7ec-5f68-9cf8-56998e6ed11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372857} {:answer/id #uuid "e56ea7f1-58ff-55ad-9ad8-ba9aac887cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} {:answer/id #uuid "11904480-c948-5232-8021-a9c3418290fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372859} {:answer/id #uuid "53b09a40-7d6a-59e1-a3c4-0d185799bac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56ea7f1-58ff-55ad-9ad8-ba9aac887cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} :answer-field/id #uuid "693c4d23-6b15-508e-aae9-7b377523a759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0189da27-d830-59de-833d-5c14386ce8c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105726" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=3Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,Z,\\,3Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,\\,3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}]}
